  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the sum of all integer bases \\( b > 9 \\) for which the number \\( 17_b \\) is a divisor of the number \\( 97_b \\).\n\n### Step 1: Convert the numbers from base \\( b \\) to base 10\nThe number \\( 17_b \\) in base \\( b \\) can be converted to base 10 as:\n\\[\n17_b = 1 \\cdot b + 7 = b + 7\n\\]\n\nThe number \\( 97_b \\) in base \\( b \\) can be converted to base 10 as:\n\\[\n97_b = 9 \\cdot b + 7 = 9b + 7\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Set up the divisibility condition\nWe need \\( 17_b \\) to be a divisor of \\( 97_b \\), which means:\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Simplify the congruence\nRearrange the congruence:\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\\[\n9b \\equiv -7 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\\[\n9b \\equiv 9b - 7 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Solve for \\( b \\)\nSince \\( 9b \\equiv 9b - 7 \\pmod{b + 7} \\), we can write:\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\\[\n9b + 7 = k(b + 7)\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\\[\n9b + 7 = kb + 7k\n\\]\n\\[\n9b - kb = 7k - 7\n\\]\n\\[\nb(9 - k) = 7(k - 1)\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{7(k - 1)}{9 - k}\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Find integer solutions for \\( b \\)\nFor \\( b \\) to be an integer, \\( \\frac{7(k - 1)}{9 - k} \\) must be an integer. This implies that \\( 9 - k \\) must divide \\( 7(k - 1) \\).\n\nLet's find the possible values of \\( k \\) such that \\( 9 - k \\) divides \\( 7(k - 1) \\).\n\n### Step 6: Test possible values of \\( k \\)\nWe test values of \\( k \\) to find integer solutions for \\( b \\):\n\n- For \\( k = 2 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(2 - 1)}{9 - 2} = \\frac{7}{7} = 1\n  \\]\n  \\( b = 1 \\) is not valid since \\( b > 9 \\).\n\n- For \\( k = 3 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(3 - 1)}{9 - 3} = \\frac{14}{6} \\approx 2.333 \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 4 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(4 - 1)}{9 - 4} = \\frac{21}{5} = 4.2 \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 5 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(5 - 1)}{9 - 5} = \\frac{28}{4} = 7 \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 6 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(6 - 1)}{9 - 6} = \\frac{35}{3} \\approx 11.666 \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 7 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(7 - 1)}{9 - 7} = \\frac{42}{2} = 21\n  \\]\n  \\( b = 21 \\) is valid since \\( b > 9 \\).\n\n- For \\( k = 8 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(8 - 1)}{9 - 8} = \\frac{49}{1} = 49\n  \\]\n  \\( b = 49 \\) is valid since \\( b > 9 \\).\n\n### Step 7: Sum the valid bases\nThe valid bases are \\( b = 21 \\) and \\( b = 49 \\). The sum of these bases is:\n\\[\n21 + 49 = 70\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{70}\n\\]",
